add('/research/briefs/','Research Briefs & Methodology | Geek Services','Read practical research briefs on AI evaluation and reproducible modeling, with reference sources and explicit limitations.','Research Briefs and Methods',p('Short technical notes for framing a question before building a system. These are educational methods, not reports of new experimental results.')+cards([('/research/briefs/evaluating-an-ai-faq/','Evaluating an AI FAQ Assistant','A repeatable approach to grounded answers, safe escalation, and human review.'),('/research/briefs/reproducible-modeling/','Making a Model Reproducible','Inputs, assumptions, baselines, and uncertainty in a useful technical model.')]))
add('/research/briefs/evaluating-an-ai-faq/','Evaluating an AI FAQ Assistant | Geek Services','A practical method for testing FAQ assistants against source material, with human escalation and documented failure cases.','Evaluating an AI FAQ Assistant',p('Research question: can a bounded assistant answer routine questions reliably enough to help a team? A fluent answer alone is insufficient evidence.')+section('Method',p('Choose approved source documents. Prepare representative questions, including questions the sources cannot answer. Record the expected answer or escalation behavior before testing. Include ambiguous requests and attempts to override the assistant’s instructions.'))+section('Measurement',p('Record whether each answer is supported, whether citations point to the right passage, and whether out-of-scope requests reach a person. Keep a separate set of unseen questions for evaluation after changes. A spreadsheet can capture question, expected behavior, observed answer, and reviewer decision.'))+section('Limits and next step',p('This method has not been run as a published experiment by Geek Services. Real deployments need privacy review, monitoring, and maintenance when source material changes.')+link('/services/ai-and-automation/','Discuss practical AI and automation'))+section('Reference',link('https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework','NIST AI Risk Management Framework')),'Research / Method brief')
add('/research/briefs/reproducible-modeling/','Making a Model Reproducible | Geek Services','Document model inputs, assumptions, baselines, and uncertainty before interpreting simulation results.','Making a Model Reproducible',p('A simulation is useful only when someone can understand what it assumes and how to repeat it. This note outlines a documentation method for analytical projects such as solar-panel configuration studies.')+section('Record the experiment',p('State the question and output units. Save input sources, retrieval dates, software versions, and parameter choices. Separate measured inputs from assumed values. For a solar model, record location, tilt, azimuth, time interval, and weather-data source.'))+section('Compare and test sensitivity',p('Use a simple baseline before adding complexity. Change one input at a time and record how much the output changes. A difference between models may reflect different assumptions rather than a better design.'))+section('Publish enough to audit',p('Provide input definitions, execution steps, limitations, and clearly labeled plots. Do not describe simulated output as a field measurement. No new simulation or numerical finding is reported in this brief.')+link('/research/projects/solar-panel-optimization/','Read the solar modeling project summary')+p(link('/services/analytics-and-reporting/','Discuss analytics and reporting')))+section('Reference',link('https://pvwatts.nrel.gov/','NREL PVWatts methodology and calculator')),'Research / Method brief')
add('/founder/','Leonardo Sotomayor, Albany Technical Founder | Geek Services','Meet Leonardo Sotomayor, founder of Geek Services, studying Informatics and AI/ML at the University at Albany.','Leonardo Sotomayor',p('Founder of Geek Services, an Albany-based applied technology and research startup. Leonardo studies Informatics with an AI/ML concentration at the University at Albany and works across software, analytical research, and practical digital systems.')+section('Technical work with a practical purpose',p('Originally from Yonkers, Leonardo brings an interest in software development, language systems, mathematical modeling, and cybersecurity to business-facing technical problems. His selected explorations are documented with their current limitations.')+link('/research/projects/','Explore Leonardo’s technical projects'))+section('Background and tools',p('His existing portfolio lists Java, Python, C++, SQL, LaTeX, Git, and TensorFlow. Academic recognition includes the 2025 Dr. Seth Spellman, Jr. Academic Achievement Award and Dean’s List.')+link('/assets/Leonardo-Sotomayor-Resume.pdf','View the founder résumé'))+section('Connect',link('https://github.com/leonardosotomayor','Leonardo on GitHub')+p(link('https://www.linkedin.com/in/leonardo-sotomayor-4b88a6356','Leonardo on LinkedIn'))),'Founder / Albany, New York')
articles=[
('how-albany-small-businesses-can-improve-website-inquiries','How Albany Small Businesses Can Improve Website Inquiries','Help visitors understand the offer and send a useful request without unnecessary steps.','websites-and-digital-systems','albany',[
('Start with one visitor task','A person comparing Albany service businesses often needs to know whether you handle their problem, serve their area, and have a clear next step. Put those answers before long company background. Read your homepage on a phone and try to find the inquiry path without using the navigation.'),
('Ask for context you will actually use','A name, email, service interest, location, and short description usually provide a useful starting point. Avoid collecting sensitive information or detailed documents before you know what is needed. Explain what happens after submission and show a confirmation.'),
('Test the complete handoff','Check required fields, delivery, and the confirmation page. Assign someone to review incoming requests. Count qualified inquiries over a consistent period and note changes to traffic or service availability before attributing an improvement to the website.')]),
('practical-digital-systems-for-capital-region-businesses','Practical Digital Systems for Capital Region Businesses','A workflow-first approach to connecting intake, task ownership, and reporting.','business-technology','albany',[
('Map one recurring process','For a Capital Region business or community organization, start with a recurring process such as a service request or event inquiry. Write down where it arrives, who acts, and what information gets copied into another tool. Ask the people doing the work where delays happen.'),
('Define the smallest useful system','Choose a shared record with an owner, a status, and a next action. Then decide whether notifications or integrations are necessary. A system that requires constant duplicate entry is unlikely to remain accurate. Access permissions and an export path belong in the initial scope.'),
('Check the operating cost','Document subscription charges, maintenance needs, and the person responsible when an integration fails. Review the workflow with the team after launch. Measure the same task before and after the change, using comparable work rather than a best-case demonstration.')]),
('what-an-albany-business-website-should-make-easy','What an Albany Business Website Should Make Easy','A practical checklist for service information, mobile navigation, and contact.','websites-and-digital-systems','albany',[
('Know whether the business is a fit','State the service, the audience, and the actual service area. Albany should appear where it helps a visitor judge relevance, rather than being repeated in every sentence. Give each major service its own page with a clear scope and links to related information.'),
('Act from a phone','Use readable type, visible focus states, and descriptive link labels. Keep contact buttons easy to reach. Use form labels that remain visible while typing, and explain errors without clearing entered details. Test with a keyboard as well as a phone.'),
('Understand the next step','Explain whether the visitor is requesting a quote, sending a question, or booking a conversation. Confirm successful form delivery and avoid implying that a request guarantees availability. Review broken links, outdated offers, and inaccurate hours as part of regular maintenance.')]),
('how-albany-businesses-can-improve-lead-follow-up','How Albany Businesses Can Improve Lead Follow-Up','Define ownership, response expectations, and a simple review routine for incoming inquiries.','ai-and-automation','albany',[
('Give each inquiry an owner','When an Albany service team is working away from a desk, an inquiry can arrive without anyone knowing who should respond. Record the channel, the time received, and the person responsible for the next action. Keep a fallback owner for absences.'),
('Separate acknowledgment from an answer','An automatic receipt can tell a visitor their message arrived, but it should not promise a response time the team cannot meet. A useful follow-up answers the actual question or requests the missing detail. Avoid repeatedly messaging someone who has declined or not consented to ongoing communication.'),
('Review unanswered requests','Use a small set of statuses such as new, contacted, waiting, and closed. Review aging requests on a schedule that fits operations. Compare qualified inquiries and completed conversations over time. Automate reminders only after the manual workflow is clear.')]),
('remote-website-improvements-for-yonkers-businesses','Remote Website Improvements for Yonkers Businesses','Scope a focused website and follow-up project for a Yonkers or Westchester business.','local-starter-pack','yonkers',[
('Define the remote deliverable','Choose a specific problem such as an unclear service page or a contact form that collects too little context. Share the current URL and describe who handles inquiries. A short, bounded project is easier to review remotely than an undefined promise of more leads.'),
('Prepare the handoff','Use separate collaborator access where tools support it, rather than sending passwords. Agree on who approves copy, checks submissions, and owns the system after delivery. Clearly state the actual Westchester service area without inventing office locations.'),
('Verify after launch','Test a real inquiry with the business owner, confirm the message arrives, and check the customer confirmation. Review the inquiry fields and follow-up routine together. Geek Services provides this work remotely from its primary Albany base.')])]
