add(demo,'Panel Orientation Geometry Demo | Geek Services','Explore a simplified solar-noon panel alignment model with adjustable latitude and tilt, a labeled chart, and published assumptions.','Explore Panel Orientation Geometry',p('An educational prototype created for this site. This illustrates one geometric component of solar modeling; it does not reproduce the founder’s original study or estimate electricity production.')+'''<div class="model"><label>Latitude north: <output id="latitude-value">43</output>°<input id="latitude" type="range" min="0" max="65" step="1" value="43"></label><label>South-facing panel tilt: <output id="tilt-value">43</output>°<input id="tilt" type="range" min="0" max="90" step="1" value="43"></label><p>Relative direct-beam alignment: <strong id="alignment">100.0%</strong></p><svg viewBox="0 0 560 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="plot-title plot-desc"><title id="plot-title">Relative alignment versus panel tilt</title><desc id="plot-desc">At latitude 43 degrees north, alignment at solar noon on the equinox peaks at a south-facing tilt of 43 degrees. This is geometry, not an annual energy forecast.</desc><path d="M50 20 V220 H530" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"/><path id="alignment-curve" fill="none" stroke="#4f6637" stroke-width="3"/><circle id="alignment-point" r="6" fill="#202521"/><text x="45" y="244">0°</text><text x="480" y="244">90° tilt</text><text x="6" y="25">100%</text><text x="18" y="220">0%</text></svg><p class="fine" id="model-status" role="status">Adjust the controls to draw the curve. JavaScript is needed for the interactive chart.</p></div>'''+section('Assumptions and equation',p('For a south-facing panel in the northern hemisphere at solar noon on an idealized equinox, solar declination is zero and the angle between the panel normal and sunlight is latitude minus tilt. The relative direct-beam projection is max(0, cos((latitude − tilt) × π / 180)). The chart samples every integer tilt from 0° to 90°.')+p('The model ignores weather, clouds, atmospheric losses, shading, temperature, diffuse light, equipment efficiency, day length, and seasonal variation. Its peak is not an annual optimal installation angle. The latitude default of 43° is an illustrative rounded value near Albany.'))+section('Reproduce and compare',link('/assets/orientation_model.py','Read or download the Python model')+p(link('https://gml.noaa.gov/grad/solcalc/','NOAA solar calculations — background geometry'))+p(link('https://pvwatts.nrel.gov/','Use NREL PVWatts for a more complete production model'))+p(link('/research/projects/solar-panel-optimization/','Return to the solar configuration project'))),'Research / Interactive educational prototype')
